WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.223
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.223 --> 00:00:05.640
DENNIS MCLAUGHLIN: Students
come in with opinions already,

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.920
and they can say things
in a seminar that

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.920
are based upon just
their common knowledge

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.973
and not based on the readings.

00:00:13.973 --> 00:00:15.390
CURT NEWTON: Today
on Chalk Radio,

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.810
we're exploring global food
production, water, and climate

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.210
change, and the
challenge of teaching

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.468
when polarized
opinions come to class.

00:00:24.468 --> 00:00:26.010
DENNIS MCLAUGHLIN:
I'd say generally,

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.440
the students learn that
most of these issues

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.990
are more subtle and
complex than they thought

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:32.890
when they came in the class.

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:36.330
So one of the things that
happens at the end of the class

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:39.240
is, people's opinions
are usually not

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:44.865
as polarized as they
were in the beginning.

00:00:44.865 --> 00:00:46.740
CURT NEWTON: I'm Curt
Newton, your guest host

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.690
for this episode of Chalk Radio.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:53.130
At MIT, officially, I'm
director of MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:56.950
But for so many years at
MIT and in my personal life,

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:00.370
I'm also doing a lot of
work on climate change.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:03.060
So when Sarah Hansen,
your usual host,

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.090
lined up this episode dealing
with these issues that

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:10.230
are so deeply personal for me
around climate change, food

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:13.980
systems, and water, and gave
me the chance to join in,

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.370
I jumped at it.

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:19.280
With prolonged droughts
made worse by climate change

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.680
affecting food
production and ecosystems

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:24.380
all around the world,
this conversation

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.420
couldn't be more timely.

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:30.170
Our guest, Dennis McLaughlin,
is a professor in MIT'S Civil

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.120
and Environmental
Engineering department,

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:36.960
and he's the instructor for
Course 1.74 Land, Water, Food,

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.440
and Climate.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:41.050
Professor McLaughlin's
interest in hydrology and what

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:43.090
it can tell us about
our food systems

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:45.540
has been years in the making.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.220
It all started back in 1991 on
a research trip that took him

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.450
halfway around the world.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:54.360
DENNIS MCLAUGHLIN:
I went to Australia.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.220
And there were a
lot of discussions

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:00.270
at the time about what was
sustainable food production

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:03.750
and whether or not Australia
could grow more food

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:07.170
with limited water
and the connection

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:10.860
between the availability
of water and the population

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.510
and food supply.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.750
It struck me as a really
interesting application area

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:18.655
for hydrology.

00:02:18.655 --> 00:02:20.280
CURT NEWTON: Dennis
explained that some

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.310
of the big wheat-growing
areas were becoming reliant

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:27.180
on irrigation to supplement
natural rainfall with water

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.580
pumped from underground
in order to keep

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.680
the crop big and healthy.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.290
But over time, all
that irrigation

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:36.688
causes salts to
build up in the soil.

00:02:36.688 --> 00:02:38.730
DENNIS MCLAUGHLIN: You
could go out in the fields

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.220
and see near the
irrigation ditches

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.860
just white layers of
salt accumulating.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.780
It's the same kind
of problem that

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:50.440
ruined Mesopotamian
civilization millennia ago.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.280
And it wasn't like it
was a big surprise.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.190
But there was not
really a good mechanism

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:01.770
to encourage farmers to hold
back on their irrigation.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:07.260
There was a lot of interest
in changing regulations

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.780
and incentives for
farmers to have

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.330
less adverse environmental
impact, especially

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:16.170
with salinity, and to make sure
that there was enough water

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:20.040
to keep the agriculture
economy healthy and going.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.260
I mean, it's not a
reasonable solution

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.090
to say we won't have
any more farming,

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:27.630
and therefore we won't have
salt accumulation, and so on.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:31.230
So it's that balance
between sustainable

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:36.092
use and economically
productive use that's tricky.

00:03:36.092 --> 00:03:37.800
CURT NEWTON: This
experience in Australia

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.370
led to the creation of a course
on water usage and agriculture

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:42.660
and food systems.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.900
It was here where Professor
McLaughlin recognized

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:49.860
that teaching these topics can
bring about an extra challenge.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.110
DENNIS MCLAUGHLIN: One of
the interesting things about

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.630
the course, and a challenging
thing as a teacher,

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:57.120
is a lot of the
topics we talk about--

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.350
sort of like talking about
climate change, or GMOs,

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:05.010
or food waste, or
vegetarianism--

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.500
students come in with
opinions already.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.780
And they can say things
in a seminar that

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.810
are based upon just
their common knowledge

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:14.550
and not based on the readings.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:18.000
So for example, in
GMOs, the focus of most

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:21.060
of the students is,
oh, would GMOs hurt me?

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:24.180
Human health-- are they going
to cause me to have cancer

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:25.920
or whatever?

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.020
And that's really not the
scientific perspective

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:30.990
so much in GMOs, because
there's almost zero

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:35.530
evidence that any GMOs are
harmful to human health.

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:38.550
And they learn that
by reading the papers.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.950
The problem is, what do
they do to the ecosystem,

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:43.050
and what do they
do to other plants

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:45.060
that are not
genetically manipulated,

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:48.360
and what are the unintended
consequences of GMOs,

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:53.460
because GMOs are generally
designed to help the farmer be

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:55.650
more productive.

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:58.620
One of the things that happens
at the end of the class is

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:01.110
people's opinions
are usually not

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:06.780
as polarized as they
were in the beginning.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:09.090
CURT NEWTON: In my own
work on climate change,

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:10.980
I've certainly had
this experience

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:14.190
of trying to have conversations
with people coming out

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:18.370
of polarized opinions
and preconceived notions.

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:20.340
I was really curious
to hear how Dennis

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:23.670
moved beyond that in this class
and helped the students have

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:25.710
more productive
conversations to view

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:30.202
these sometimes controversial
issues from multiple angles.

00:05:30.202 --> 00:05:32.660
DENNIS MCLAUGHLIN: It's really
just an introductory course.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:35.000
And it's to kind of
stimulate interest

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:38.090
that they had when they
came in and show them

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:42.500
that it is possible to pursue
difficult topics by going

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:43.460
to the literature.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:46.490
Many of them aren't used to
reading scientific papers,

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:48.380
the undergraduates especially.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:50.000
They're used to
being lectured to

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:52.212
and things being in textbooks.

00:05:52.212 --> 00:05:54.170
CURT NEWTON: And here's
where Dennis McLaughlin

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:55.670
gets creative.

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:58.040
For each article he
assigns, a student

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:00.980
is asked to present that
article to the class.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:05.810
But Dennis also gives a
specific prompt to make sure

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:07.580
that these
presentations actually

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.067
help the students
understand the material.

00:06:10.067 --> 00:06:12.150
DENNIS MCLAUGHLIN: Another
challenge in the course

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:15.170
is to make sure that
their presentations don't

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:16.920
bore the other students.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:20.420
So I said, you just
have a minute or two,

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:22.070
and you should
pose some questions

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:24.530
for the class or some
issues and make them

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:26.630
as controversial as possible.

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:29.240
So that's what we try to do.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:31.400
CURT NEWTON: As
controversial as possible?

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:33.470
Wouldn't encouraging
controversy just

00:06:33.470 --> 00:06:37.520
prevent us from moving
beyond polarized opinions?

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.240
Dennis went on to explain
why controversy really

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:44.600
is productive and important
to deal with, especially

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:46.907
in science and
engineering classrooms.

00:06:46.907 --> 00:06:48.740
DENNIS MCLAUGHLIN:
Another thing they're not

00:06:48.740 --> 00:06:52.370
used to, the students, even
some of the graduate students,

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:54.830
is scientific controversy.

00:06:54.830 --> 00:06:58.250
There's perhaps not that
much controversy, ironically,

00:06:58.250 --> 00:07:00.140
interestingly, about GMOs.

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:02.180
But there's a lot of
controversy about things

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:05.090
like organic farming
and whether or not

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:08.060
we should be willing to
sacrifice yield a bit

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:10.650
to have more
sustainable agriculture.

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:12.650
And one of the things
we find out in the class

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:17.330
is, many people who are
organic farming advocates

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:21.650
are out of the mainstream of
the scientific literature.

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:27.260
But when you review the papers
on the other side that say,

00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:30.560
for example, it's silly
to try to continue

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:32.660
smallholder farming in Africa.

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:35.120
Africa's agricultural
future should

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:39.590
be large, industrial farms,
perhaps run by foreigners

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:41.780
who buy or lease the land.

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:44.270
Some people write
assessments like that.

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:45.950
And you say, well,
yeah, but they're

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:49.070
being funded by the large
agricultural industry.

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:50.930
Well, does that
mean they're wrong?

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:51.770
Are they biased?

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:53.540
Well, I don't know.

00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:56.090
But they become advocates, too.

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:58.730
So the students read
the papers, and we

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:05.240
try to discuss both sides of an
argument based on the merits.

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:08.720
And I'm not saying
students don't get exposure

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:10.070
to that kind of thing elsewhere.

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:11.737
But if they're taking
physics, and we're

00:08:11.737 --> 00:08:19.058
talking about Newton's law, it's
not presented as a controversy.

00:08:19.058 --> 00:08:21.320
CURT NEWTON: Motivating
students to engage

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:24.800
with the scientific literature
to unpack controversies

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:27.200
is only part of the work
involved in teaching

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:28.820
this engineering course.

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:33.362
There's something else, too,
something really important.

00:08:33.362 --> 00:08:34.820
DENNIS MCLAUGHLIN:
It's a challenge

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:38.299
to decide how much
effort and discussion

00:08:38.299 --> 00:08:42.470
to spend on learning
about scientific issues,

00:08:42.470 --> 00:08:47.450
scientific controversies,
more or less agreed-on facts,

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:50.240
and how much to spend
on things like values,

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:53.990
because you really can't talk
about smallholder farming

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:56.060
unless you talk about poverty.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:58.910
You can ask questions as to why
is the smallholder farmer so

00:08:58.910 --> 00:08:59.450
poor?

00:08:59.450 --> 00:09:02.540
Why does the smallholder kids
not want to stay on the farm?

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:04.130
They want to go to the city.

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:05.480
What do they do in the city?

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:06.980
Are there jobs there?

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:09.440
Well, do they have
any education?

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:11.330
Well, no, because the
smallholder farmer

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:15.360
doesn't have enough money
to send the child to school.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.330
What about the role
of women in farming

00:09:17.330 --> 00:09:19.310
and the role of
cultural differences

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:20.960
in how women are viewed?

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:24.590
And can they, for example,
participate in ownership?

00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:26.330
You could just spend
the whole semester

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:29.213
talking about this stuff.

00:09:29.213 --> 00:09:31.130
CURT NEWTON: Dennis's
course attracts students

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:34.190
from a wide variety of
disciplines and backgrounds.

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:37.790
And it's this diversity of
experiences among the students

00:09:37.790 --> 00:09:41.878
that enriches the class for
everyone, including Dennis.

00:09:41.878 --> 00:09:43.295
DENNIS MCLAUGHLIN:
I had a student

00:09:43.295 --> 00:09:49.760
in the last class who was an art
student at Mass College of Art.

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:54.860
And she knew almost nothing
about engineering or science,

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:59.390
but she came from Venezuela
and settled in Puerto Rico,

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:01.400
and her aunt is a farmer.

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:06.590
And so she talked about how hard
it was for Puerto Rican farmers

00:10:06.590 --> 00:10:09.260
to be competing with Central
American farmers, who

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:11.870
were very poor,
who were exporting

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:13.770
produce to Puerto Rico.

00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:16.310
So Puerto Rico,
this place where you

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:21.350
can grow food all the time,
this very productive climate,

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:25.610
is not producing much food
for its own residents because

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:26.510
of pricing.

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:29.660
And she actually brought that to
the class and talked about it.

00:10:29.660 --> 00:10:32.400
And I wouldn't have
even thought about it.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:35.840
So there's this interaction
between the students who

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:41.120
have had farming experience or
just a student from Puerto Rico

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:43.100
who's talking to one
from Philadelphia,

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:46.280
and they're both from cities,
but they have a much different

00:10:46.280 --> 00:10:48.300
perspective.

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:49.850
So not only did
the other students

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:53.723
learn from these students,
but I learned from them.

00:10:53.723 --> 00:10:55.640
CURT NEWTON: As you may
have picked up by now,

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:59.190
climate change is an important
topic in this course.

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:01.820
We asked Dennis how
climate change will

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:04.123
affect food and agriculture.

00:11:04.123 --> 00:11:06.290
DENNIS MCLAUGHLIN: Well,
that sounds like a question

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:07.820
we could pose in the class.

00:11:07.820 --> 00:11:12.310
And we do discuss it
through reading papers.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:16.400
So this is a big topic
and a controversial one.

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:19.780
One thing that there seems to
be pretty good agreement on

00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:23.680
is if climate change
increases temperatures

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:25.870
during the growing
season sufficiently, that

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:27.790
will hurt agriculture.

00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:31.220
But it depends on the
crop, depends on the place.

00:11:31.220 --> 00:11:34.540
It's a lot different talking
about small increases

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:38.860
in average temperature
versus large increases

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:42.820
that could actually reduce
yield or even kill crops.

00:11:42.820 --> 00:11:47.118
So temperature extremes
probably won't help.

00:11:47.118 --> 00:11:49.410
CURT NEWTON: This course
really pushes its participants

00:11:49.410 --> 00:11:51.990
to look beyond their
own assumptions.

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:55.290
Questions, contradictions,
uncertainties--

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:58.170
those are inevitable when
we take on topics as complex

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:01.480
as food, water,
and climate change.

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:03.480
Professor McLaughlin
embraces that

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:06.000
questioning as a core
part of the class,

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.058
even as he tries to
provide some answers.

00:12:09.058 --> 00:12:11.100
DENNIS MCLAUGHLIN: Especially
undergrads, they're

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:13.830
used to going into class and
thinking they're learning,

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:16.120
quote, facts, and that's
the end of the story.

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:20.880
But I view the teacher as
kind of adjusting, resetting

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:24.270
the discussion to keep
things kind of on track

00:12:24.270 --> 00:12:27.390
and consistent with the papers.

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:31.740
But giving the students the
freedom to say silly things

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:34.530
or to go beyond the
scope of the paper

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:37.110
is part of why they
like the course.

00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:40.530
When they learned that there
are differences of opinion,

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:42.660
even among experts
and that there

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:45.900
are values, value
judgments, and that there's

00:12:45.900 --> 00:12:50.260
social equity, and
all kinds of things--

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:54.090
they don't even connect that,
really, with formal education.

00:12:54.090 --> 00:12:56.190
But it's not so easy
because, for one thing,

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:59.230
it takes the students
off balance a bit.

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:01.860
So it's definitely an
art, and you learn it

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:03.540
over multiple times.

00:13:03.540 --> 00:13:06.870
You can't just start
the very first year

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:09.120
and be perfect on it.

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:12.240
So you have to learn the
teaching style as well

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:13.350
as the content.

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:16.812
You have to teach yourself how
to be observant to what works

00:13:16.812 --> 00:13:17.520
and what doesn't.

00:13:20.277 --> 00:13:22.360
CURT NEWTON: Maybe we could
all take a page or two

00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:25.225
from Professor McLaughlin
and his students

00:13:25.225 --> 00:13:26.950
and get a little
more comfortable

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:30.670
being knocked off balance.

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:33.790
Dennis' perspective made me
wonder how other educators

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:35.950
handle similar situations.

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:38.320
How do you integrate
conversations

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:41.890
about values and culture into
your science and engineering

00:13:41.890 --> 00:13:45.310
courses in a way
that feels balanced?

00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:48.760
You can share your insights
via the link in the show notes.

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:52.600
And we'll pass them along to
MIT faculty and listeners, who

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:54.730
could incorporate your
ideas into their teaching.

00:13:57.658 --> 00:13:59.200
If you're interested
in learning more

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:01.030
about Professor
McLaughlin's work

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:04.450
or teaching with his open
educational resources,

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:08.750
you can check out his course,
1.74 Land, Water, Food,

00:14:08.750 --> 00:14:12.860
and Climate, on our MIT
OpenCourseWare website.

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:17.920
It's one of over 150 courses
in OCW's extensive environment

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:19.420
courses collection.

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:23.050
And for even more, check
out the MIT Climate website

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:27.670
at climate.MIT.edu, where
you'll find a wealth of material

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:30.500
spanning these topics and more.

00:14:30.500 --> 00:14:32.480
Thank you so much for listening.

